## Condition for a Left-Invariant Metric on a Lie Group to be Bi-Invariant

**Theorem.** Let $G$ be a Lie group with Lie algebra $\mathfrak g = T_eG$, and let $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ be an inner product on $\mathfrak g$, determining a left-invariant Riemannian metric on $G$. The following are equivalent:

1. The metric is **bi-invariant** (both left- and right-invariant).
2. The inner product is **$\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant**:
$$\langle \operatorname{Ad}_g X,\, \operatorname{Ad}_g Y\rangle = \langle X,Y\rangle \qquad \forall\, g\in G,\; X,Y\in\mathfrak g.$$
3. The inner product is **$\operatorname{ad}$-invariant** (infinitesimally $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant):
$$\langle [Z,X],\, Y\rangle + \langle X,\, [Z,Y]\rangle = 0 \qquad \forall\, X,Y,Z\in\mathfrak g,$$
equivalently, $\operatorname{ad}_Z$ is **skew-adjoint** with respect to $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ for every $Z\in\mathfrak g$, or in cyclic form
$$\langle [X,Y], Z\rangle = \langle X, [Y,Z]\rangle \qquad \forall\, X,Y,Z\in\mathfrak g.$$

---

### Proof

**Setup.** A left-invariant metric on $G$ is uniquely determined by an inner product $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ on $\mathfrak g$, via
$$\langle u,v\rangle_g := \langle (dL_{g^{-1}})_g u,\, (dL_{g^{-1}})_g v\rangle, \qquad u,v\in T_gG.$$
Left-invariance is built in by construction. The metric is bi-invariant iff it is *also* right-invariant, i.e.
$$\langle (dR_g)_e X,\, (dR_g)_e Y\rangle = \langle X,Y\rangle \qquad \forall\, g\in G,\; X,Y\in\mathfrak g. \tag{$\ast$}$$

**(1) $\Leftrightarrow$ (2): bi-invariance $\Leftrightarrow$ $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariance.**

Recall $\operatorname{Ad}_g = (dC_g)_e$ where $C_g(h)=ghg^{-1}=L_g\circ R_{g^{-1}}(h)$. Hence
$$\operatorname{Ad}_g X = (dL_g)_e\,(dR_{g^{-1}})_e X.$$
Replacing $g$ by $g^{-1}$ and solving,
$$(dR_g)_e X = (dL_g)_e\,\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}} X.$$
Now compute the right-hand side of $(\ast)$ using left-invariance of the metric:
$$\langle (dR_g)_e X,\, (dR_g)_e Y\rangle = \big\langle (dL_g)_e\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}X,\,(dL_g)_e\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}Y\big\rangle = \langle \operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}X,\,\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}Y\rangle,$$
where the last equality is exactly left-invariance applied at the identity. Therefore $(\ast)$ holds iff
$$\langle \operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}X,\,\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}Y\rangle = \langle X,Y\rangle \qquad \forall\,g,X,Y.$$
Since $g\mapsto g^{-1}$ ranges over all of $G$, this is equivalent to
$$\langle \operatorname{Ad}_g X,\,\operatorname{Ad}_g Y\rangle = \langle X,Y\rangle \qquad \forall\,g\in G,\;X,Y\in\mathfrak g,$$
which is precisely $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariance. $\square$

**(2) $\Leftrightarrow$ (3): $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariance $\Leftrightarrow$ $\operatorname{ad}$-invariance.**

($\Rightarrow$) Assume $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariance. Fix $Z\in\mathfrak g$ and set $g=\exp(tZ)$. Differentiating
$$\langle \operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}X,\,\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}Y\rangle = \langle X,Y\rangle$$
at $t=0$ gives, using $\frac{d}{dt}\big|_{0}\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)} = \operatorname{ad}_Z$,
$$\langle \operatorname{ad}_Z X,\, Y\rangle + \langle X,\, \operatorname{ad}_Z Y\rangle = 0,$$
i.e. $\langle [Z,X],Y\rangle + \langle X,[Z,Y]\rangle = 0$. This is the $\operatorname{ad}$-invariance condition.

($\Leftarrow$) Assume $\operatorname{ad}$-invariance. For any $Z\in\mathfrak g$ define
$$\varphi(t) := \langle \operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}X,\,\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}Y\rangle.$$
Then
$$\varphi'(t) = \langle \operatorname{ad}_Z\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}X,\,\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}Y\rangle + \langle \operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}X,\,\operatorname{ad}_Z\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}Y\rangle.$$
By $\operatorname{ad}$-invariance (with $Z'=Z$, $X'=\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}X$, $Y'=\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(tZ)}Y$), the two terms cancel, so $\varphi'(t)=0$. Hence $\varphi(t)\equiv\varphi(0)=\langle X,Y\rangle$, giving $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariance for all $g$ in the image of $\exp$.

Since every $g\in G$ (connected component of $e$) is a finite product of exponentials, and $\operatorname{Ad}$ is a homomorphism with $\operatorname{Ad}_{g_1g_2}=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_1}\operatorname{Ad}_{g_2}$, the invariance extends to all of $G^0$. (On a connected Lie group this is all of $G$; on a disconnected group one additionally requires invariance under each component, which is the standard formulation.) $\square$

**Cyclic form.** The condition $\langle [Z,X],Y\rangle+\langle X,[Z,Y]\rangle=0$ rewrites, using antisymmetry of the bracket, as
$$\langle [X,Y],Z\rangle = \langle X,[Y,Z]\rangle = \langle [Z,X],Y\rangle,$$
the fully cyclic form, confirming $\operatorname{ad}_Z^\ast = -\operatorname{ad}_Z$.

---

### Summary

A left-invariant metric on a Lie group $G$ (given by an inner product on $\mathfrak g$) is bi-invariant **if and only if** the inner product is $\operatorname{Ad}(G)$-invariant, equivalently, **iff $\operatorname{ad}_X$ is skew-adjoint for every $X\in\mathfrak g$**:
$$\boxed{\;\langle [X,Y],Z\rangle = \langle X,[Y,Z]\rangle \quad \forall\, X,Y,Z\in\mathfrak g.\;}$$

A standard consequence: under a bi-invariant metric the geodesics through $e$ are exactly the one-parameter subgroups, $\nabla_X Y = \tfrac12[X,Y]$ for left-invariant fields, and $\operatorname{ad}_X$ skew-adjoint implies the sectional curvature formula $K(X,Y)=\tfrac14\|[X,Y]\|^2/\big(\|X\|^2\|Y\|^2-\langle X,Y\rangle^2\big)\ge 0$ for compact-type groups.

